          "The published Western Cape schedule is explicit about devices. Under the line item covering assistive devices and prostheses, H0 patients pay nothing, H1 patients pay 10% of the full cost and H2 patients pay 20% ([Western Cape Provincial Gazette 8902 of 2024](https://www.westerncape.gov.za/department-premier/files/wcg-blob-files?file=2026-02%2F8902-Extra-Uniform-Patient-Fee-2024.pdf&type=file)). The income bands are consistent across provinces: for the tariffs Gauteng introduced on 1 April 2025, H1 covers individuals earning under R70,000 a year and households under R100,000, H2 runs up to R250,000 for an individual and R350,000 for a household, and above those figures you are H3 and pay the full fee ([Gauteng Department of Health](https://www.gov.za/news/media-statements/gauteng-health-revised-uniform-patient-fee-tariffs-17-mar-2025)). The same 2025 research estimates that roughly 70% to 80% of the population qualifies as fully subsidised, and is therefore eligible to receive a prosthesis free of charge through the public system ([Visagie et al.](https://www.scielo.org.za/scielo.php?pid=S2226-72202025000100096&script=sci_arttext)).",
